"""
prepare(raw_dir, public_dir, private_dir) -> None

Reads dataset/raw/works.jsonl (one OpenAlex work per line: work_id, title,
abstract, topic_id, topic_name, field_name, domain_name, publication_year)
and builds the public/private split for this challenge.

Design
------
Each test row asks: given a query work's abstract, and six candidate
"exemplar groups" (one of which shares the query's true OpenAlex primary
topic, five of which are other real topics), which group does the query
belong to? Each candidate group is shown only as one other work's
abstract — never as a topic name or any human-readable label string — so
the task cannot be solved by string-matching a descriptive label against
the query text. The solver must judge topical similarity between the
query abstract and each candidate's example abstract directly.

Split is GROUP-DISJOINT by topic_id: every topic_id is entirely in train or
entirely in test, never both. This is required because exemplars shown
alongside a test query must never include that same work, and must not be
drawn from a topic whose other members leak into training.

Distractor topics for a given query are drawn from a fixed similarity band
(ranks HARD_NEIGHBOR_RANK_LO..HARD_NEIGHBOR_RANK_HI) of that topic's nearest
neighbors, precomputed by a pretrained sentence encoder over topic-centroid
embeddings (dataset/raw/hard_neighbors.json, built by
build_hard_distractors.py). Same-domain-random distractors were measured and
found too easy (a stock encoder scored 0.75-0.80, far above the target
agent band of 0.2-0.6); nearest-neighbor topics are harder to separate
because they are chosen by actual topical closeness rather than coarse
domain membership. The closest 1-2 ranks are excluded from the band to
avoid rows whose "distractor" is arguably indistinguishable from the true
topic (see DESIGN.md for the measured effect of each band).

This script is deterministic and seeded: two runs over the same raw/ must
produce byte-identical public/ and private/ files.
"""
